lling or expert ratings yet exist for the 2028 race. With the incumbent president term-limited, both parties face open contests, and early national polling shows a competitive 2028 field led by figures such as JD Vance and Kamala Harris.\n\nKansas is expected to favor Republicans by about seven points, reflecting the state's durable partisan lean, tempered slightly by the open race and a national climate that favored Democrats in the 2026 midterms. Uncertainty remains wide because no Kansas-specific surveys have been conducted this cycle. A strong Democratic nominee or a further shift in the national environment could put the state in play, but current evidence points right of center.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1844, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2276, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2132, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2313, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.227, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2252, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2505, "margin": -7.0}]}